# Line endings, pinned.
#
# Git for Windows installs with core.autocrlf=true by default, so without
# this file a Windows checkout rewrites every text file to CRLF on the way
# out of the object store. For source that is only ever compiled or parsed
# that is harmless. For a fixture that is compared BYTE FOR BYTE it is not:
# the checked-in bytes and the bytes the code produces stop being the same
# bytes, and the test fails on every line for a reason that has nothing to
# do with the code under test.
#
# The concrete case (issue #143) is
# packages/codeindex/go/testdata/goldencorpus/.golden/symbols_edges.txt.
# The scanner renders its canonical document with "\n"; os.ReadFile hands
# the test whatever is on disk. On a CRLF checkout every one of its 109
# lines differs, and the diff blames the scanner.
#
# eol=lf is the working-tree side of the contract; the repository has
# always stored LF. Nothing here changes what is committed on a checkout
# that was already LF -- it changes what Windows is handed.

# Default: let Git detect text vs binary, and check text out as LF on
# every platform. There are no .bat/.cmd/.ps1 files in this repo, which
# are the usual reason to exempt something from this rule.
* text=auto eol=lf

# --- byte-compared fixtures -------------------------------------------
# These are not merely "text files that happen to work"; a test reads them
# and compares bytes, or the file is the recorded output of another tool
# and its value is that it is untouched. Marked explicitly so the intent
# survives any future edit to the default rule above.

# The Go golden corpus: the snapshot itself, and the sources it is a
# snapshot OF. TestGoldenCorpus_LineEndingsDoNotMoveTheSnapshot asserts
# the scan is CRLF-insensitive, so the sources are pinned for
# reproducibility rather than correctness; the snapshot is pinned because
# TestGoldenCorpus_SymbolsAndEdgesMatchSnapshot compares it byte for byte.
packages/codeindex/go/testdata/goldencorpus/**  text eol=lf

# Recorded `go test -coverprofile` output. The gocover parser tolerates a
# trailing CR (bufio.ScanLines strips it, strings.Fields splits on it),
# so this is preventive: these files are cited in comments as the
# untouched output of a real tool run, and a checkout that rewrites them
# makes that claim false.
*.coverprofile                                  text eol=lf
packages/coverage/testdata/**                   text eol=lf
packages/cfg/testdata/**                        text eol=lf
test/acceptance/testdata/**                     text eol=lf

# --- binary ------------------------------------------------------------
# Stated rather than left to Git's heuristic.
*.png                                           binary
*.db                                            binary
